# Security tool pitfalls

Rules for intrusion detectors, lockout controls, device admission, and
tamper-evident logs. Each is a defect class that passes review and fails in
production.

## Alert noise is a security defect

A rule that fires on ordinary activity trains the user to ignore the tool. The
classic: alert on device attach. Plugging in a phone is not an intrusion, and
controller root-hubs re-enumerate on every boot.

- Alert only on a genuine boundary crossing by someone else: authentication
  failure, privilege escalation attempt, unauthorized device admission.
- Move policy for benign events from *alerting* to *enforcing*. Device policy
  belongs in a blocking layer (udev/AD), not a notification.
- Exempt the local admin's own activity explicitly, by actor name, not by
  keyword. A rule matching the bare word `faillock` fires on the admin's own
  `sudo cp /etc/security/faillock.conf`.

## Debounce must not swallow sustained attacks

A long debounce window means a password-spraying run produces exactly one
alert — the case where repeat notification matters most. Shorten the window and
shorten it further for critical severity, rather than dropping events.

## A fullscreen takeover is the wrong default

An alert that hijacks the display is indistinguishable from one triggered by
the user's own activity, and it obstructs the response. Default to a desktop
notification; make any fullscreen display an explicit opt-in flag.

Notification delivery must degrade loudly, not silently:

- A root-owned `notify-send` has no D-Bus connection and can exit 0 having
  shown nothing. Drop to the desktop user and supply `DISPLAY` /
  `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`.
- If every delivery route fails, log `ALERT DELIVERY FAILED` even though the
  policy was applied. Silence here is the one failure mode that must never be
  swallowed.

## Never discard a subprocess return code on a security path

`subprocess.run(...)` without checking `returncode` makes a failed evidence
write indistinguishable from a successful one. The break stays invisible until
the proof is needed — which is the worst possible time.

## Exemptions must be as narrow as the threat

Granting an exemption via a *group* exempts every current and future member.
Choosing a widely-populated group for a "single admin" exemption silently
converts it into "everyone with that privilege", including any account an
attacker persuaded the owner to create.

- Use a dedicated single-member group for a single-account exemption.
- Guard enrollment so membership is a deliberate, visible act, not a side
  effect of unrelated admin work.
- Check the exemption **interface** class, not the device class, when deciding
  what to spare — HID devices commonly report device class `00` rather than the
  expected HID class, so a device-class check misses the very case it was meant
  to spare.
- Prefer lengthening an unlock timeout over exempting an account outright when
  the goal is "don't lock me out"; the exemption is only needed when the
  requirement is absolute.

## Enforcement must be real, and must fail safe

"Quarantine" that only writes a log entry is not quarantine. Unbinding the
driver that grants disk or network access is.

- Write an explicit never-block list for the things that would lock the owner
  out (root hubs, input devices), and make the installer warn when a currently
  attached device is missing from the allow list.
- Ship the uninstall path in the same file as the install path.
- Install a policy file root-owned and non-writable by the invoking user; a
  writable allow list is a disabled allow list.

## Tamper-evident chains: an anchor is a snapshot

The most dangerous bug in this class: verification compares the sealed anchor
against the **live** head. Because records are legitimately appended after a
seal, this reports tampering on every new event. A monitor that cries wolf about
tampering is worse than no monitor — the operator concludes the evidence was
altered when it was not.

Validate only the prefix the anchor witnessed:

- `sealed_count > len(chain)` → records were removed
- `chain[sealed_count-1].h != sealed_head` → the sealed prefix was altered
- records beyond `sealed_count` → normal, already covered by per-record MACs

Then test **both** directions, or the fix is unverified:

| Injected fault | Must be caught |
|---|---|
| Edit a sealed record's contents | per-record MAC mismatch |
| Delete a record from the middle | sequence gap + prev-hash break |
| Truncate the chain | anchor count exceeds chain length |
| Nothing (clean chain, new record) | must report INTACT |

A verification path that has only ever been exercised on a clean chain proves
nothing.

## Confirm directives against the real man page

Before writing a system config directive, check the installed documentation:

```bash
zcat /usr/share/man/man5/<name>.5.gz | col -b > /tmp/doc.txt
grep -A4 '<directive>' /tmp/doc.txt
```

`strings` on the implementing shared object is a useful cross-check but is not
authoritative — a valid directive may be absent from the string table, and a
directive's *type* (group vs user, name vs path) is the thing that actually
bites. Read the semantics, not just the existence.

## State the tradeoff once

Security exemptions carry real cost: an exempt account is not protected from
online guessing by that mechanism. Say so concretely, with the mechanism and
the remaining controls, then implement the requested behavior. Do not repeat
the caveat in every subsequent message, and do not omit it.
